\section{summary()}{

\code{summary()} of a workflow fit returns an object of class \code{summary.} plus
the fit's class, such as \code{summary.contentvalid_sort}. It prints the counts
by status, the scale- or panel-level evidence, and a "Flagged" section
with a sentence for each unit to look at again. The object is a list
holding, among others, \code{n_items}, \code{n_supported}, \code{n_review},
\code{n_insufficient}, \code{n_descriptive}, \code{scale_summary}, \code{reviewed_items} (the
units whose status is \code{Review} or \verb{Insufficient data}), \code{settings}, and
\code{design}. The Delphi summary adds \code{panel} and \code{stability_method}; the
expert-panel summary \code{mode} and, when an agreement coefficient was
computed, \code{agreement}; the judge summary \code{gtheory}, \code{influence_items}, and
\code{reviewed_judges}; and the coverage summary \code{gaps}, the cells under
review.

\code{summary()} of a \code{\link[=compare_rounds]{compare_rounds()}} result returns a
\code{summary.contentvalid_rounds} list whose \code{changed} holds the units that
changed status between the first and last rounds, entered or left, or
appeared only in between, with the counts in \code{summary}.
}

\section{plot()}{

Figures are drawn with base graphics, and each has its own help page:
\code{\link[=plot.contentvalid_sort]{plot.contentvalid_sort()}}, \code{\link[=plot.contentvalid_rating]{plot.contentvalid_rating()}},
\code{\link[=plot.contentvalid_expert]{plot.contentvalid_expert()}}, \code{\link[=plot.contentvalid_delphi]{plot.contentvalid_delphi()}},
\code{\link[=plot.contentvalid_evidence]{plot.contentvalid_evidence()}}, \code{\link[=plot.contentvalid_structure]{plot.contentvalid_structure()}},
\code{\link[=plot.contentvalid_sort_power]{plot.contentvalid_sort_power()}}, and \code{\link[=plot.contentvalid_expert_power]{plot.contentvalid_expert_power()}}.

A judge-heterogeneity fit, a coverage analysis, and a comparison of rounds
have no figure. Their \code{plot()} methods stop with a message that says where
to look instead. A coverage analysis given similarity data draws its
content map with \code{plot(x$details$structure)}.
}

\section{as.data.frame()}{

A workflow fit returns its \code{results} or its \code{scale_summary}
(\code{\link[=as.data.frame.contentvalid_workflow]{as.data.frame.contentvalid_workflow()}}). A component index returns the
plain data frame, list, or matrix it holds, coerced to a data frame,
without its print class. \code{\link[=content_report]{content_report()}} returns its table of text,
and \code{\link[=content_evidence]{content_evidence()}} its long table of evidence, one row per item and
stage. The other results are covered in \link{contentvalid-data-frames}.
}

